End a scan its query no longer needs.

The reader of a scan waited for the server inside libpq (PQexec for the
COPY to begin, PQgetCopyData for each row), where nothing looks for the
query's interrupt, and nothing ended a COPY the reader left unread: libpq
reads the rest of it before the connection takes its next command, the
COMMIT or ROLLBACK that ends the query included. A query that was canceled,
that failed, or that had the rows it needed (a LIMIT above a filter, a
semi-join) went on until the server had sent every row of every scan.

The reader now waits for the server itself, a tenth of a second at a time,
and throws once the query is interrupted. When it is destroyed with its
COPY unfinished, it has the server cancel the COPY, as psql does on Ctrl-C,
and reads to the end of the command, which the cancel brings: the
connection is ready for its next command when the scan is gone. So does
any command sent on a connection whose COPY is still in flight, such as
the ROLLBACK of a session that ends inside a transaction, which DuckDB
sends before it destroys the query's readers. The request goes to the
server process of that connection alone, and is repeated each second the
command goes on, as one that arrives before the command is lost. A server
that takes no cancel requests is read to the end, as before. A wait that a
signal ends early goes on: the runtime of a Go program signals its threads,
the one that runs the query among them.

